  "authoringNote": "No final current usable encounter exists: encounter-a is restricted and encounter-b is a usable draft. C09 applies R04 candidates whenever no final current usable encounter exists, so the usable draft never hides the restricted version, which may be the final one. R04 therefore holds both R04.encounter_restricted naming encounter-a, which sets the finding to restricted for the authorization coordinator, and R04.encounter_not_final naming encounter-b, which keeps its own missing, authorization-coordinator, needs_information routing beneath the selected status. The C07 parent names only encounter-a, the restricted condition at the finding's status. Under the recorded reading (a rule cites evidence-field facts only from the documents it reads; R04 reads only final current usable documents), R04 cites exactly the final order's status, date and practitioner identifier (three): no draft status, date or practitioner fact and nothing from the restricted version. The draft states no LCMP incorporation, so no LCMP predicate holds. R09 gives encounter-a R09.access_restricted and names it as its parent. Rules that read drafts use encounter-b (C05, C09): R02 cites its beneficiary name and identifier with the order and receipt (six), R06 cites its five clinical statements as evidence candidates for qualified review with no administrative effect, and R07 passes citing its false LCMP flag. R01 (eight), R03 (nine) and R05 (four; the receipt follows the order date span under C06) pass, R08 passes and R10 is not applicable. Availability does not disappear behind the draft, and no negative clinical assertion is made about the restricted record. The state is needs_information.",
